  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "When proving that the interior angles of a triangle sum to \\(180^\\circ\\), a line parallel to \\(BC\\) is drawn through vertex \\(A\\). Which fact is mainly used?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "When two lines are parallel, alternate interior angles are equal",
    "B": "When two lines are parallel, interior angles on the same side of a transversal are supplementary",
    "C": "Vertically opposite angles are equal",
    "D": "An exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two nonadjacent interior angles"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Draw \\(DE\\parallel BC\\) through \\(A\\). Then \\(\\angle B=\\angle BAD\\) and \\(\\angle C=\\angle CAE\\) (alternate interior angles are equal). Together with \\(\\angle BAC\\), they form a straight angle of \\(180^\\circ\\).",
